[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124293,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The solar lentigines of solar lentigo are ultraviolet (UV) radiation&ndash;related benign intraepidermal keratinocytic proliferations, closely related to seborrhoeic keratoses.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124294,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>8052/0 Solar lentigo</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124295,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>EJ20.1 &amp; XH7B58 Actinic lentigo &amp; Solar lentigo</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124296,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Acceptable:</em> actinic lentigo.</p>\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> senile lentigo.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124297,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124298,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chronically sun-exposed skin, particularly of the face, the forearms, and the dorsal aspects of the hands, is affected.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124299,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Patients with solar lentigines present with small and uniformly pigmented tan to brown macules, measuring a few millimetres in diameter. Darkly pigmented examples are also referred to as ink-spot lentigines.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124300,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Solar lentigines are markers of chronic UV radiation exposure. Their incidence increases with age, and onset is typically in middle-aged to elderly adults with fair skin {15140067}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124301,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Chronic UV radiation exposure is the main etiological factor {15140067}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124302,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Little is known about the pathogenesis of these keratinocytic proliferations. Like in seborrhoeic keratoses, mutations in the <em>FGFR3</em> and <em>PIK3CA</em> genes have been detected in a number of cases {19076977}. Solar lentigines appear to lack <em>BRAF</em> mutations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124303,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>See <em>Clinical features</em>, above.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 124304,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 15,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Solar lentigines are well-demarcated intraepidermal proliferations of bland basaloid keratinocytes, characterized by elongated rete ridges with hyperpigmentation of the basal cell layer and budding at the base. There may be fusion of rete ridges, overlying hyperkeratosis, and mildly increased numbers of junctional melanocytes {9091478}. Solar lentigines typically arise on a background on solar elastosis. Ink-spot lentigines are small and sharply circumscribed, with intense melanin pigmentation of basal cell layer keratinocytes that is most prominent at the tips of the rete ridges.</p>\r\n<p>Solar lentigines may show some morphological overlap with seborrhoeic keratosis, and reliable separation is not always possible. Pigmented actinic keratoses are characterized by squamous dysplasia. Lentigo maligna is typically associated with loss of the rete ridge pattern and shows confluent growth of atypical melanocytes, best highlighted by immunohistochemistry for melan-A, SOX10, or MITF {21797920}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-24T14:15:05"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
